<Docs
  title="Security"
  description="The trust boundaries of karucode: what the file tools contain, what the shell does not, how the server is reached from another machine, and what to set before you expose it."
  path="/security/"
  section="Security"
  lede="The trust boundaries, and the one that is missing."
  toc={TOC}
  edit={`${SITE.repo}/blob/main/docs/SECURITY.md`}
  prev={{ href: '/docs/configuration/', title: 'Configuration' }}
  next={{ href: '/docs/', title: 'Docs overview' }}
>
  <p>
    karucode is a coding agent, not a sandbox. It confines its own file tools to a workspace. It does
    not confine the programs you ask it to run, and it does not pretend to. This page is a spec: read
    it before you bind the server to anything other than loopback, and before you leave a run
    unattended.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-is-contained">What is contained</h2>
  <p>
    Every file tool resolves its path through a confined workspace before it touches anything:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>No <code>..</code> escapes and no absolute paths outside the workspace root.</li>
    <li>Symlink escapes are canonicalised away, so a link cannot be used to step outside.</li>
    <li>Writes are atomic: a file is replaced, never left half-written.</li>
    <li>
      The system temp dir is a second root by default. Turn that off with
      <code>KARUCODE_ALLOW_TEMP_DIR=false</code> if you want the workspace to be the only place the
      file tools can reach.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    New project roots are unrestricted by default. <code>KARUCODE_PROJECT_ROOTS</code> is a
    colon-separated allowlist if you want to fix that too.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-is-not">What is not</h2>
  <Callout kind="danger" title="The shell is not sandboxed">
    <p>
      A shell command runs as the server user, with that user’s permissions, its files, its network
      reach and its environment. <strong>Approvals are the gate, not isolation.</strong> Approving a
      command is the decision to let it run as you.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the workspace is untrusted, or if you run unattended <code>auto</code> mode, run karucode
      inside a container or a virtual machine. That boundary is yours to provide; the binary does not
      add one.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>Three more limits worth stating plainly:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Plugins and MCP servers are trusted code.</strong> They are separate processes with no
      filesystem sandbox. A rebuilt plugin is reloaded — <code>POST /api/plugins/reload</code> or
      <code>plugins_watch</code> — not restarted with the server.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Subagents share the parent’s live workspace</strong>, including dirty and untracked
      files. <code>owned_paths</code> is advisory conflict reporting, not a lock: two agents can still
      write the same file.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>There is no TLS in the binary.</strong> For remote access, terminate TLS in a reverse
      proxy in front of karucode.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="reaching-it-from-another-machine">Reaching it from another machine</h2>
  <p>
    By default the server binds to <code>127.0.0.1</code>. A non-loopback bind is refused unless
    <code>KARUCODE_AUTH_TOKEN</code> is set to at least 16 characters, or <code>[access]</code> is
    enabled and backed by a real password or passkey — the bind, not the request, is what needs
    the credential.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>KARUCODE_ALLOWED_HOSTS</code> extends the <code>Host</code> allowlist. An entry can be an
    exact host, a <code>host:port</code>, or a <code>*.domain</code> wildcard — and the wildcard does
    not match the bare domain, so <code>*.example.com</code> does not admit
    <code>example.com</code>.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap kc-host">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Bind</th>
          <th scope="col">Token</th>
          <th scope="col">What is served</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>loopback</td>
          <td>unset</td>
          <td>
            Only the Hosts <code>127.0.0.1</code>, <code>localhost</code> and <code>[::1]</code>, plus
            anything in <code>KARUCODE_ALLOWED_HOSTS</code> or <code>[access] public_url</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>loopback</td>
          <td>set</td>
          <td>
            The Host allowlist check is skipped, but the <code>Origin == Host</code> rule stays.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>non-loopback</td>
          <td>unset</td>
          <td>The bind is refused, unless sign-in is enabled with a password or passkey.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>non-loopback</td>
          <td>set</td>
          <td>Serves with the bearer token, and still without TLS.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The consequence is worth knowing: with a token configured, the Host check is skipped, so the
    credential is what stands between a hostile page and your server — not the name it used to reach
    it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="signing-in">Signing in</h2>
  <p>
    A bearer token is awkward in a browser: a page cannot put a header on the document that runs the
    app. So the web UI has its own sign-in — a <strong>password</strong> or a
    <strong>passkey</strong> — exchanged for an <code>HttpOnly</code> session cookie. Both work
    alongside the token; neither replaces it.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Password:</strong> hashed with Argon2id, verified in constant time. Only the hash is
      stored, in the SQLite settings table — never in the config file, never in
      <code>GET /api/config</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sessions:</strong> 32 random bytes in the cookie; the server stores only the SHA-256,
      so a copy of the database cannot be turned into a login. <code>SameSite=Lax</code>,
      <code>HttpOnly</code>, and <code>Secure</code> when the request arrived over TLS. Signing out
      deletes the row.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Lockout:</strong> eight wrong attempts from one address within 15 minutes lock that
      address out for five minutes — including the correct password.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Passkeys:</strong> ES256 only (RSA and Ed25519 are refused at registration). The
      challenge, origin, relying-party id, user-present flag and signature counter are all checked;
      the attestation statement is not, so Touch ID, Windows Hello, a phone and a YubiKey all work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Behind a proxy:</strong> only the direct peer is compared against
      <code>trusted_proxies</code>, so a client cannot claim to be loopback by sending
      <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>. <code>tunnel = "cloudflare"</code> trusts Cloudflare’s published
      ranges.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The first-run wizard sets this up in one step: model endpoint, approval mode, and the
    password or passkey. A passkey is bound to the host it was registered on, so set
    <code>public_url</code> before registering the one you plan to use remotely.
  </p>

  <h2 id="in-the-browser">In the browser</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>CSRF:</strong> browser clients are protected by a custom header plus the
      <code>Origin</code>/<code>Host</code> check — and, once sign-in is on, by a
      <code>SameSite=Lax</code> session cookie. A cross-site form post can set none of them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>DNS rebinding:</strong> with a loopback bind and no token, only <code>127.0.0.1</code>,
      <code>localhost</code> and <code>[::1]</code> Hosts are served (plus your explicit allowlist and
      <code>[access] public_url</code>), so a hostile domain that resolves to your machine is still
      rejected by name. The guard is <em>not</em> skipped when sign-in is enabled: a cookie, unlike a
      bearer header, is sent automatically.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Secrets:</strong> never returned to the browser and never written to logs. The provider
      API key is write-only: you can set it, you cannot read it back.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Changing the dangerous things:</strong> approvals and the provider endpoint require
      <code>KARUCODE_ADMIN_TOKEN</code> — a second, separate secret from the auth token.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-the-model-cannot-take-with-it">What the model cannot take with it</h2>
  <p>
    Child processes get a credential-free environment allowlist. A prompt-injected <code>curl</code>
    that dumps the environment gets neither the harness token nor the provider key.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is a statement about the environment, not a network policy: an approved command can still
    reach the network with whatever access the server user has, and file tools can still read the
    workspace. It closes the cheapest exfiltration path, not all of them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rendered-content">Rendered content</h2>
  <p>
    Model output is not trusted markup. Markdown is rendered with raw HTML disabled and then
    sanitised, so a document in the transcript cannot inject script into the page.
  </p>
  <p>
    A chat artifact is stronger than that: it is a separate document in a sandboxed opaque-origin
    frame, addressed by a random per-artifact capability — never the master token. Script network
    access, remote subresources and forms are blocked inside it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="before-you-expose-it">Before you expose it</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Put a credential in front of it: <code>KARUCODE_AUTH_TOKEN</code> of at least 16 characters, or
      a password/passkey with <code>[access] enabled</code>. Without one, a non-loopback bind is
      refused.
    </li>
    <li>
      Set <code>KARUCODE_ADMIN_TOKEN</code>, so changing approvals or the endpoint needs a second
      secret.
    </li>
    <li>Put a reverse proxy in front of it. The binary terminates no TLS.</li>
    <li>
      Run it in a container or a VM if the workspace is untrusted, or if you use
      <code>auto</code> mode.
    </li>
    <li>
      Back up the SQLite database in <code>KARUCODE_DATA_DIR</code>. Every transcript, run and agent
      record is in it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Set <code>KARUCODE_LOCK_SETTINGS=1</code> if you want settings pinned by the environment, with
      browser edits rejected <code>403</code>.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <a href={`${SITE.repo}/blob/main/docs/SECURITY.md`} class="kc-readmore">
      Read the full security notes
      <svg
        viewBox="0 0 12 12"
        width="12"
        height="12"
        aria-hidden="true"
        fill="none"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="1.2"
        stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round">
        <path d="M3.5 8.5 8.5 3.5M4.75 3.5h3.75v3.75" />
      </svg>
    </a>
  </p>
  <p class="kc-footnote">
    Found something that contradicts this page? The source of truth is
    <code>docs/SECURITY.md</code> in the repository, and issues are public.
  </p>
</Docs>

